analysisWe need to output an assert statement corresponding to comment: "// @@@ If the element is not null, then the result is true."

We are in method add(final E element) returns boolean true always. The comment says: If the element is not null, then the result is true.

We have a precondition: element != null. The method already checks Objects.requireNonNull(element) which throws NPE if null. So the spec: (\old(element != null)) => \result == true? Actually element is a parameter, not changed. So we can express: element != null => \result == true. Since result is boolean, true is boolean literal. So assert element != null => \result; (since \result is true). But we need to use boolean expression.

Thus final assert: assert element != null => \result; but need parentheses maybe: assert (element != null) => \result; In code: <code>assert (element != null) => \result;</code>

Check syntax: Use => binary with two operands, lower precedence than &&. So parentheses around left side is fine.

